11:["$","section",null,{"className":"reference-section battle-flow-section","id":"battle-flow","children":["$","$L1e",null,{"guide":{"date":"March 22–23, 1862","place":"Winchester and Kernstown, Virginia; the Valley Turnpike, Pritchard’s Hill, Barton’s Woods, Middle Road, and Sandy Ridge in the lower Shenandoah Valley","result":"This episode covers only the opening of the First Battle of Kernstown. Nathan Kimball concentrated a much larger United States force around Pritchard’s Hill, checked Turner Ashby’s morning probes, and kept the field command despite suggestions from the wounded James Shields in Winchester. Jackson arrived near noon, attacked on the basis of Ashby’s false estimate, and shifted infantry and artillery west toward Sandy Ridge without fully explaining his plan to his brigade commanders. The episode ends when Sandie Pendleton reports the scale of the United States force and Jackson acknowledges the danger. The later Union counterattack, Confederate retreat, and United States tactical victory are outside this installment","facts":[{"label":"Episode scope","value":"The March 22 skirmish and the opening phase of First Kernstown on March 23","note":"The narrative stops before Erastus Tyler’s main attack on Sandy Ridge, Richard Garnett’s retreat, and the end of the battle. Those events belong to episode 143."},{"label":"Overall battle result","value":"United States tactical victory; later events not covered here","note":"The National Park Service also describes a Confederate strategic benefit: the battle helped persuade Washington to keep or redirect troops toward the Valley rather than McClellan’s movement against Richmond. That consequence cannot be inferred from the incomplete action heard in this episode alone."},{"label":"Immediate cause","value":"Jackson attacked on false intelligence","note":"Turner Ashby reported only four United States infantry regiments and a battery or two near Winchester. In fact, Shields’s division remained in the area. Ashby then encountered evidence of the larger force on March 23 but did not send Jackson a corrective report before the main column arrived."},{"label":"Forces","value":"Roughly 3,500 Confederates against roughly 8,000–8,500 United States troops","note":"Totals vary with the definition of present versus engaged. The episode estimates that straggling left Jackson about 3,000 men on arrival. Jackson’s official report lists 3,087 infantry present that evening, 2,742 engaged, 27 guns with 18 engaged, and 290 cavalry. Shields reported that the United States force did not exceed 7,000, while the NPS order of battle rounds the division to about 8,500."},{"label":"Shields wounded","value":"Shell fragment fractured his left arm on March 22","note":"James Shields was taken to Winchester. He continued receiving reports and sending discretionary suggestions, but Colonel Nathan Kimball exercised field command on March 23. Shields survived the wound."},{"label":"United States field commander","value":"Col. Nathan Kimball","note":"Kimball commanded the First Brigade of Shields’s division. He declined repeated suggestions to advance prematurely because he suspected that additional Confederate forces were approaching."},{"label":"First shot","value":"About 9 a.m., March 23","note":"One of Ashby’s guns opened the battle after an 8th Ohio reconnaissance identified Confederate cavalry, infantry, and artillery south of Kernstown."},{"label":"Key United States position","value":"Pritchard’s Hill","note":"The hill stood west of the Valley Turnpike and commanded the approaches south of Winchester. Kimball placed ten rifled guns there, supported first by the 67th Ohio and 5th Ohio and later by additional infantry."},{"label":"Morning Confederate force","value":"Ashby’s cavalry, artillery, and four companies of the 2nd Virginia","note":"The infantry detachment was led by Capt. John Q. A. Nadenbousch. Ashby tried both sides of the Valley Turnpike but was pushed back southeast of Kernstown by about 11 a.m."},{"label":"Jackson’s arrival","value":"Near noon, about two miles south of Kernstown","note":"The episode estimates that most of the infantry had marched more than 20 miles on March 22 and at least 12 additional miles on March 23. Jackson allowed the arriving brigades to rest before reconnoitering."},{"label":"Decision to fight on Sunday","value":"Jackson decided that delay was more dangerous than an immediate attack","note":"The episode quotes Jackson’s later letter to his wife explaining his religious misgivings. The transcript’s word “circumcised” is a speech-recognition error; published transcriptions render the phrase as “circumstanced as on that day.”"},{"label":"Jackson’s plan","value":"Move west, seize Sandy Ridge, turn the United States right, and reach the Valley Turnpike","note":"The transcript briefly renders the feature as “Sandy Bridge,” but the battlefield feature is Sandy Ridge. The route was intended to avoid a direct assault on the guns at Pritchard’s Hill."},{"label":"Barton’s Woods","value":"Covered approach used by Fulkerson and the Stonewall Brigade","note":"Fulkerson’s 23rd and 37th Virginia reached the northern edge of the woods about 2 p.m. The Stonewall Brigade followed, except for the 5th Virginia, which Jackson retained near the turnpike."},{"label":"Artillery reorganization","value":"Jackson detached brigade batteries into a reserve under his direct orders","note":"Burks was directed to support the guns while the 48th Virginia guarded the wagon and ammunition trains. Later in the episode, eleven Confederate guns unlimbered on the exposed eastern slope of Sandy Ridge."},{"label":"Command-information failure","value":"The brigade commanders received movements without Jackson’s overall plan","note":"Garnett later complained that he lacked the information needed to act intelligently. Fulkerson, Garnett, and nearby regimental commanders repeatedly made decisions without knowing which friendly units were moving toward Sandy Ridge."},{"label":"Fulkerson’s exposed advance","value":"About 600 men of the 37th and 23rd Virginia crossed open ground","note":"Instead of passing safely around the United States position, the column moved toward a grove at the western base of Pritchard’s Hill. Fire from the hill, Battery L of the 1st Ohio, and the appearance of the 84th Pennsylvania caused Fulkerson to turn left toward Sandy Ridge."},{"label":"Stonewall Brigade deployment","value":"The 33rd Virginia initially advanced without most of the brigade","note":"Garnett and Colonel Arthur C. Cummings followed Fulkerson with about 275 men. The order had not reached the 2nd, 4th, and 27th Virginia, which remained in Barton’s Woods until Jackson sent Sandie Pendleton to move them."},{"label":"Ashby’s diversion","value":"About 150 cavalrymen attacked east of the turnpike around 2:30 p.m.","note":"Ashby overran an 8th Ohio skirmish company before being repulsed with one man killed and six wounded. The demonstration helped keep the equivalent of five United States regiments east of the turnpike."},{"label":"Artillery disadvantage","value":"United States rifled guns held higher ground and greater effective range","note":"The episode places the Pritchard’s Hill guns about one mile away and roughly 100 feet above the exposed Confederate batteries. Jackson told Sergeant Clement Fishburne to reduce fire from his short-ranged 12-pounder howitzer."},{"label":"“We are in for it”","value":"Attributed to Jackson after Pendleton estimated at least 10,000 opponents","note":"The episode presents the exchange as a remembered command anecdote. It captures Jackson’s late recognition that the initial intelligence was wrong, but the exact wording should not be treated as a contemporaneous transcript."},{"label":"Important leader deaths in this installment","value":"None","note":"Shields was seriously wounded but survived. The episode mentions one Confederate cavalryman killed during Ashby’s diversion, but no important commander was killed or mortally wounded in the action covered here."},{"label":"Book recommendation","value":"“We Are in for It!”: The First Battle of Kernstown, March 23, 1862 by Gary L. Ecelbarger","note":"The automated transcript misspells Ecelbarger’s surname. The book was published by White Mane in 1997."}],"maps":[{"title":"Morning: Kimball secures Pritchard’s Hill","caption":"After an 8th Ohio reconnaissance found Ashby south of Kernstown, a Confederate gun opened fire about 9 a.m. Kimball placed ten rifled guns and supporting infantry on Pritchard’s Hill. Ashby’s movement east of the turnpike met the 14th Indiana and other United States troops, and by about 11 a.m. his force had been pushed southeast. The stream course, routes, positions, and arrows are schematic; the map does not claim exact unit frontages.","source":"https://www.nps.gov/articles/000/first-battle-of-kernstown.htm","image":"/images/ep142-kernstown-morning.svg","alt":"Schematic morning map of First Kernstown showing Pritchard’s Hill, Valley Turnpike, United States guns, the 8th Ohio reconnaissance, and Ashby’s Confederate probes"},{"title":"Early afternoon: Jackson shifts toward Sandy Ridge","caption":"Fulkerson moved through Barton’s Woods and into the meadow, then turned left under fire toward Sandy Ridge. Garnett followed with the 33rd Virginia before Jackson sent the rest of the brigade and artillery west. Eleven Confederate guns formed on the exposed eastern slope while Ashby demonstrated east of the Valley Turnpike. This is a phase diagram based on the episode, NPS narrative, and period maps; distances, routes, and unit locations are approximate.","source":"https://www.nps.gov/articles/000/first-battle-of-kernstown.htm","image":"/images/ep142-kernstown-afternoon.svg","alt":"Schematic early-afternoon map of First Kernstown showing Barton’s Woods, Sandy Ridge, Pritchard’s Hill, Confederate brigade movements, artillery positions, and Ashby’s diversion"}],"moments":[{"time":"0:30","audioTime":30,"title":"Soldiers introduce the close-range battle","detail":"The episode opens with accounts by Sgt. Frank B. Nickerson of the 8th Ohio and Sgt. Clement Fishburne of the Rockbridge Artillery. Nickerson’s encounter previews fighting completed in the next episode; Fishburne’s artillery experience appears later in this one."},{"time":"2:57","audioTime":177,"title":"The March 22 situation is recapped","detail":"Jackson had marched north after Ashby reported that Banks’s force was leaving the Valley. Ashby was already skirmishing near Kernstown."},{"time":"3:58","audioTime":238,"title":"Ashby’s shell disables James Shields","detail":"A shell fragment broke Shields’s left arm above the elbow. He was carried back to Winchester and could not direct the battle in person."},{"time":"6:00","audioTime":360,"title":"Nathan Kimball takes field command","detail":"As the senior brigade commander present, Kimball assumed control and drove Ashby’s small force south toward Newtown before nightfall."},{"time":"6:37","audioTime":397,"title":"Both commands misread the skirmish","detail":"Banks and Shields still believed Jackson was distant, while Jackson accepted Ashby’s report that only four United States regiments remained near Winchester."},{"time":"8:20","audioTime":500,"title":"Straggling reduces Jackson’s column","detail":"Hundreds had fallen out during the March 22 forced march. The episode estimates that fewer than 3,500 men reached the Strasburg area."},{"time":"9:03","audioTime":543,"title":"Four infantry companies reinforce Ashby","detail":"At first light on March 23 Jackson sent four companies of the 2nd Virginia ahead. The rest of the exhausted command did not start for nearly two more hours."},{"time":"9:19","audioTime":559,"title":"The 8th Ohio conducts a reconnaissance","detail":"Lt. Col. Franklin Sawyer led companies west of the turnpike, crossed Pritchard’s Hill, and identified Confederate guns, dismounted cavalry, and infantry before withdrawing."},{"time":"10:47","audioTime":647,"title":"The battle’s first shot is fired","detail":"At about 9 a.m. one of Ashby’s guns opened fire. The shell caused no damage but prompted Kimball to concentrate his force."},{"time":"11:02","audioTime":662,"title":"Kimball identifies the key ground","detail":"Pritchard’s Hill commanded the Valley Turnpike and the surrounding approaches. Kimball made it the center of his defensive position."},{"time":"11:28","audioTime":688,"title":"Ten rifled guns occupy Pritchard’s Hill","detail":"The artillery could reach Confederate movement along the turnpike and across the open ground to the west."},{"time":"11:35","audioTime":695,"title":"The 67th Ohio clears the hill’s base","detail":"Kimball sent the inexperienced regiment into woods west of the hill, then placed it behind the guns. The 5th Ohio also moved up in support."},{"time":"12:29","audioTime":749,"title":"Ashby tries the east side of the turnpike","detail":"Capt. John Q. A. Nadenbousch moved his four 2nd Virginia companies north, hoping to outflank Pritchard’s Hill."},{"time":"13:13","audioTime":793,"title":"Kimball sends the 14th Indiana to meet the probe","detail":"From Pritchard’s Hill, Kimball saw the Confederate movement and reinforced the United States troops east of the turnpike shortly before 10 a.m."},{"time":"13:25","audioTime":805,"title":"Ashby recognizes the larger force but sends no warning","detail":"He recalled Nadenbousch after encountering much more resistance than expected, yet did not correct his earlier report to Jackson."},{"time":"13:49","audioTime":829,"title":"Ashby is contained southeast of Kernstown","detail":"By about 11 a.m. his force occupied woods roughly half a mile southeast of the hamlet. United States skirmishers halted in the muddy fields to the north."},{"time":"14:28","audioTime":868,"title":"Jackson expects to reach Winchester","detail":"Before marching that morning, he had told Joseph E. Johnston that he hoped to be near Winchester that evening."},{"time":"14:50","audioTime":890,"title":"Banks leaves for Washington","detail":"An aide reported that only the same limited Confederate force seen on March 22 appeared to be present. Banks therefore continued with his planned departure."},{"time":"15:38","audioTime":938,"title":"Shields sends suggestions from Winchester","detail":"The wounded division commander read reports and dictated messages through surgeon H. M. Maccabee, but left tactical execution to Kimball in the field."},{"time":"16:55","audioTime":1015,"title":"Kimball refuses a premature advance","detail":"He rejected a suggestion to push up the Valley Turnpike because he suspected that more Confederate troops were near and preferred to strengthen his position."},{"time":"19:00","audioTime":1140,"title":"Jackson reaches the battlefield area","detail":"After the advertisement break, the narrative resumes near noon. The Stonewall, Fulkerson, and Burks brigades halted about two miles south of Kernstown to rest."},{"time":"20:02","audioTime":1202,"title":"Jackson reconnoiters with his aides","detail":"He rode forward with George Junkin and Sandie Pendleton. He initially preferred to wait until Monday but had fewer than six hours of daylight and believed the visible guns could report his strength."},{"time":"20:26","audioTime":1226,"title":"The visible guns conceal the larger position","detail":"Jackson could see the ten guns on Pritchard’s Hill but not all the supporting infantry. He also failed to confer with Ashby about the morning’s fighting east of the turnpike."},{"time":"21:04","audioTime":1264,"title":"Jackson orders an immediate attack","detail":"He feared delay would allow the United States rear guard to escape or receive reinforcements. His later letter to his wife described the decision to fight on Sunday as a distasteful duty."},{"time":"22:02","audioTime":1322,"title":"The plan turns toward Sandy Ridge","detail":"Jackson intended to move west, turn the United States right at Pritchard’s Hill, reach the Valley Turnpike in the rear, and open the route to Winchester."},{"time":"22:30","audioTime":1350,"title":"Fulkerson enters Barton’s Woods","detail":"The 23rd and 37th Virginia led the covered movement north. They reached the woods’ northern edge about 2 p.m."},{"time":"23:07","audioTime":1387,"title":"Jackson centralizes the artillery","detail":"He detached batteries from their brigades and placed them under his direct orders. Burks supplied infantry support while the 48th Virginia guarded the trains."},{"time":"24:12","audioTime":1452,"title":"Brigade commanders lack the full plan","detail":"Orders passed through Pendleton and Junkin, but Garnett, Fulkerson, and Burks were not told Jackson’s overall purpose or the complete disposition of friendly units."},{"time":"25:03","audioTime":1503,"title":"Kimball again chooses defense","detail":"At about 1:30 p.m. another suggestion arrived from Shields to attack. Kimball had seen Confederate movement in Barton’s Woods and held his ground."},{"time":"25:56","audioTime":1556,"title":"Pritchard’s Hill opens on Barton’s Woods","detail":"The rifled guns fired on Confederates at the northern edge of the timber as Jackson tried to organize the western movement."},{"time":"26:35","audioTime":1595,"title":"Fulkerson receives an exposed assignment","detail":"Jackson ordered him to turn the guns’ flank, requiring the two-regiment brigade to cross roughly three-quarters of a mile of open and obstructed ground."},{"time":"27:31","audioTime":1651,"title":"About 600 Confederates enter the meadow","detail":"The 37th Virginia led and the 23rd Virginia followed in a narrow column. Marshy ground, fences, and artillery fire slowed the movement."},{"time":"28:13","audioTime":1693,"title":"Battery L and the 84th Pennsylvania block the route","detail":"Capt. Lucius Robinson’s 1st Ohio battery unlimbered northwest of the grove, while the 84th Pennsylvania formed near it. Fulkerson turned left toward Sandy Ridge."},{"time":"28:48","audioTime":1728,"title":"Only the 33rd Virginia follows at first","detail":"Garnett accompanied Colonel Arthur C. Cummings and about 275 men into the meadow. The rest of the Stonewall Brigade had not received the movement order."},{"time":"29:36","audioTime":1776,"title":"Garnett also turns toward Sandy Ridge","detail":"Concentrated artillery fire and lack of information caused him to redirect the 33rd Virginia west rather than continue toward Pritchard’s Hill."},{"time":"30:10","audioTime":1810,"title":"Garnett considers withdrawing","detail":"With Fulkerson out of sight and the rest of his brigade absent, he believed the small force on Sandy Ridge could accomplish little and might be counterattacked."},{"time":"30:50","audioTime":1850,"title":"Jackson starts the remaining regiments forward","detail":"He sent Pendleton to move the 2nd and 4th Virginia. They crossed the meadow under artillery fire by about 2:30 p.m.; the 27th Virginia and more guns followed."},{"time":"31:50","audioTime":1910,"title":"Ashby makes a diversion east of the turnpike","detail":"About 150 cavalrymen crossed the stream, overran an 8th Ohio skirmish company, and were repulsed with one killed and six wounded. Kimball retained substantial strength on that flank."},{"time":"33:42","audioTime":2022,"title":"Garnett and Fulkerson still lack a common picture","detail":"They discussed returning to Barton’s Woods without knowing that additional Confederate infantry and artillery were already moving toward them."},{"time":"34:20","audioTime":2060,"title":"Eleven Confederate guns unlimber on Sandy Ridge","detail":"The batteries were exposed on the eastern slope, below and about a mile from the rifled guns on Pritchard’s Hill. United States fire immediately struck men and horses."},{"time":"35:39","audioTime":2139,"title":"Jackson reaches the Confederate batteries","detail":"Fishburne’s 12-pounder howitzer could not reach the United States position effectively. Jackson ordered only occasional fire while the crew sheltered behind a stone fence."},{"time":"37:01","audioTime":2221,"title":"Pendleton reports the real scale of the opposition","detail":"From higher ground on Sandy Ridge, he saw extensive United States infantry and artillery and estimated at least 10,000 men between Jackson and Winchester."},{"time":"37:59","audioTime":2279,"title":"Jackson says, “We are in for it”","detail":"He told Pendleton to keep the information quiet, fearing its effect on the command. The episode ends its battle narrative at this moment of recognition."},{"time":"38:09","audioTime":2289,"title":"The hosts recommend Ecelbarger’s battle study","detail":"They recommend Gary L. Ecelbarger’s “We Are in for It!” for a detailed study of the First Battle of Kernstown."}],"command":[{"side":"United States","color":"#315f8c","branches":[{"commander":"Maj. Gen. Nathaniel P. Banks","command":"V Corps, Army of the Potomac","subordinates":"Retained overall responsibility for the Valley force but left Winchester for Washington after receiving reassuring reports on March 23"},{"commander":"Brig. Gen. James Shields","command":"Second Division, V Corps","subordinates":"Wounded on March 22; received battlefield reports in Winchester and sent discretionary suggestions while Kimball commanded in the field"},{"commander":"Col. Nathan Kimball","command":"First Brigade and acting field commander","subordinates":"14th Indiana, 8th Ohio, 67th Ohio, and 84th Pennsylvania; concentrated the defense at Pritchard’s Hill and controlled the division on the battlefield"},{"commander":"Col. Jeremiah C. Sullivan","command":"Second Brigade, Shields’s division","subordinates":"5th Ohio, 13th Indiana, 62nd Ohio, and 39th Illinois; the 39th Illinois is listed by NPS as unengaged"},{"commander":"Col. Erastus B. Tyler","command":"Third Brigade, Shields’s division","subordinates":"7th Ohio, 7th Indiana, 1st Virginia (later West Virginia), 29th Ohio, and 110th Pennsylvania; his main attack occurs after this episode ends"},{"commander":"Lt. Col. Philip Daum","command":"Division artillery","subordinates":"United States and Ohio batteries, including ten rifled pieces concentrated on Pritchard’s Hill during the opening phase"}]},{"side":"Confederacy","color":"#9a3d32","branches":[{"commander":"Gen. Joseph E. Johnston","command":"Department of Northern Virginia","subordinates":"Ordered Jackson to keep United States troops in the Valley and away from the main movement against Richmond"},{"commander":"Maj. Gen. Thomas J. “Stonewall” Jackson","command":"Valley District","subordinates":"Directed three infantry brigades, Ashby’s cavalry, and the available artillery; ordered the attack and personally controlled the artillery reserve"},{"commander":"Brig. Gen. Richard B. Garnett","command":"Stonewall Brigade","subordinates":"2nd, 4th, 5th, 27th, and 33rd Virginia Infantry with associated batteries; the 5th remained near the turnpike during the western movement"},{"commander":"Col. Jesse S. Burks","command":"Second Brigade","subordinates":"21st, 42nd, and 48th Virginia Infantry and 1st Virginia (Irish) Battalion; the 48th guarded the trains"},{"commander":"Col. Samuel V. Fulkerson","command":"Third Brigade","subordinates":"23rd and 37th Virginia Infantry; led the movement through Barton’s Woods and across the meadow toward Sandy Ridge"},{"commander":"Col. Turner Ashby","command":"7th Virginia Cavalry and attached forces","subordinates":"Conducted the morning action south and east of Kernstown, failed to correct his earlier intelligence, and led the afternoon diversion east of the turnpike"}]}],"people":[{"name":"Thomas J. “Stonewall” Jackson","role":"Valley District commander who attacked on faulty intelligence and shifted his main effort west toward Sandy Ridge","side":"Confederacy","image":"/images/ep142-stonewall-jackson.jpg","source":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2003674500/"},{"name":"Nathan Kimball","role":"First Brigade commander who assumed field command, concentrated the United States defense, and resisted pressure to advance prematurely","side":"United States","image":"/images/ep142-nathan-kimball.jpg","source":"https://www.loc.gov/pictures/item/2018666446/"},{"name":"James Shields","role":"Division commander disabled by a shell wound on March 22; monitored the March 23 action from Winchester","side":"United States"},{"name":"Turner Ashby","role":"Cavalry commander whose false estimate led Jackson forward and who failed to report the stronger opposition encountered on March 23","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"Richard B. Garnett","role":"Commanded the Stonewall Brigade and tried to interpret incomplete orders while its regiments moved piecemeal toward Sandy Ridge","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"Samuel V. Fulkerson","role":"Led the 23rd and 37th Virginia through Barton’s Woods, across the exposed meadow, and toward Sandy Ridge","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"Jesse S. Burks","role":"Commanded the Confederate brigade assigned to support Jackson’s centralized artillery and protect the trains","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"William N. “Sandie” Pendleton","role":"Jackson staff officer who transmitted movement orders, then reconnoitered from Sandy Ridge and reported the scale of the United States force","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"George Junkin","role":"Jackson aide who accompanied the reconnaissance and carried orders during the early-afternoon deployment","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"John Q. A. Nadenbousch","role":"Captain commanding the four companies of the 2nd Virginia sent to reinforce Ashby and probe east of the turnpike","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"Franklin Sawyer","role":"Lieutenant colonel of the 8th Ohio who led the morning reconnaissance across Pritchard’s Hill","side":"United States"},{"name":"Lucius N. Robinson","role":"Captain of Battery L, 1st Ohio Light Artillery, whose guns helped force Fulkerson’s column away from Pritchard’s Hill","side":"United States"},{"name":"Arthur C. Cummings","role":"Colonel of the 33rd Virginia who accompanied Garnett in the regiment’s isolated advance across the meadow","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"Clement Fishburne","role":"Rockbridge Artillery sergeant whose account describes the exposed Confederate gun line and his short-ranged howitzer","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"Nathaniel P. Banks","role":"V Corps commander who left for Washington after receiving reports that underestimated the developing Confederate attack","side":"United States"}],"losses":[],"gallery":[{"image":"/images/ep142-nathan-kimball.jpg","alt":"Wartime photographic portrait of United States officer Nathan Kimball in uniform","caption":"Nathan Kimball commanded Shields’s division in the field after Shields was wounded. He used Pritchard’s Hill as the anchor of a cautious defense and correctly anticipated that Ashby’s morning force was not acting alone.","source":"https://www.loc.gov/pictures/item/2018666446/"},{"image":"/images/ep142-stonewall-jackson.jpg","alt":"Wartime photographic portrait of Confederate general Thomas J. Stonewall Jackson in uniform","caption":"Jackson reached Kernstown believing he faced a small rear guard. By the episode’s end, he had committed his command and learned that a much larger United States force blocked the route to Winchester.","source":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2003674500/"},{"image":"/images/ep142-kernstown-sneden.jpg","alt":"Robert Knox Sneden’s color plan of the Battle of Kernstown with roads, terrain, and troop positions","caption":"Robert Knox Sneden’s wartime-era plan presents the battle’s roads, terrain, and colored troop positions. It is a historical interpretation produced between 1862 and 1865, not a surveyed map or a reliable guide to exact strength and frontage. Its troop-count annotation is substantially higher than the official reports and modern estimates.","source":"https://www.loc.gov/item/gvhs01.vhs00077/"},{"image":"/images/ep142-kernstown-morning.svg","alt":"Schematic morning map of the First Battle of Kernstown around Pritchard’s Hill and the Valley Turnpike","caption":"Kimball’s concentration on Pritchard’s Hill stopped Ashby’s morning probes and gave the United States artillery observation over the approaches from the south.","source":"https://www.nps.gov/articles/000/first-battle-of-kernstown.htm"},{"image":"/images/ep142-kernstown-afternoon.svg","alt":"Schematic early-afternoon map of Confederate movements from Barton’s Woods toward Sandy Ridge","caption":"Jackson tried to avoid a direct attack on Pritchard’s Hill by moving west, but incomplete orders and the exposed approach broke the movement into disconnected parts.","source":"https://www.nps.gov/articles/000/first-battle-of-kernstown.htm"}],"sources":[{"label":"Official Civil War Podcast episode 142 page","url":"https://civilwarpodcast.org/2016/02/14/civil-war-podcast-episode-142/"},{"label":"National Park Service: First Battle of Kernstown","url":"https://www.nps.gov/articles/000/first-battle-of-kernstown.htm"},{"label":"National Park Service: Jackson’s 1862 Shenandoah Valley Campaign","url":"https://www.nps.gov/articles/000/jackson1862-valley-campaign.htm"},{"label":"U.S. Army Center of Military History: The Virginia Campaigns, March–August 1862","url":"https://history.army.mil/portals/143/Images/Publications/catalog/75-5.pdf"},{"label":"Primary source collection: Official Records, Series I, Volume XII, Part I","url":"https://ehistory.osu.edu/books/official-records/015"},{"label":"Primary source: Shields’s report and United States strength, Official Records page 342","url":"https://ehistory.osu.edu/books/official-records/015/0342"},{"label":"Primary source: Jackson’s report and Confederate strength, Official Records page 383","url":"https://ehistory.osu.edu/books/official-records/015/0383"},{"label":"Primary source: Nathan Kimball, “Fighting Jackson at Kernstown,” Battles and Leaders, volume II","url":"https://ehistory.osu.edu/books/battles/vol2/302"},{"label":"Shenandoah Valley Battlefields National Historic District: First Kernstown","url":"https://www.shenandoahatwar.org/first-kernstown-article"},{"label":"Library of Congress: Robert Knox Sneden plan of the Battle of Kernstown","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/gvhs01.vhs00077/"},{"label":"Library of Congress: Nathan Kimball portrait","url":"https://www.loc.gov/pictures/item/2018666446/"},{"label":"Library of Congress: Thomas J. “Stonewall” Jackson portrait","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2003674500/"},{"label":"White Mane: Gary L. Ecelbarger, “We Are in for It!”","url":"https://www.whitemane.com/we-are-in-for-it-the-first-battle-of-kernstown"}]}}]}]
12:["$","section",null,{"className":"reference-section","id":"people","children":[["$","h2",null,{"children":"People and command"}],[[["$","link","css:/projects/civil-war-field-companion/_next/static/css/page.m4AAmS8w.css",{"rel":"stylesheet","precedence":"vite-rsc/importer-resources","href":"/projects/civil-war-field-companion/_next/static/css/page.m4AAmS8w.css","data-rsc-css-href":"/projects/civil-war-field-companion/_next/static/css/page.m4AAmS8w.css"}],["$","link","css:/projects/civil-war-field-companion/_next/static/css/index.B0pjVDS7.css",{"rel":"stylesheet","precedence":"vite-rsc/importer-resources","href":"/projects/civil-war-field-companion/_next/static/css/index.B0pjVDS7.css","data-rsc-css-href":"/projects/civil-war-field-companion/_next/static/css/index.B0pjVDS7.css"}],["$","link","css:/projects/civil-war-field-companion/_next/static/css/command-trees.KCNQiAeH.css",{"rel":"stylesheet","precedence":"vite-rsc/importer-resources","href":"/projects/civil-war-field-companion/_next/static/css/command-trees.KCNQiAeH.css","data-rsc-css-href":"/projects/civil-war-field-companion/_next/static/css/command-trees.KCNQiAeH.css"}],"$undefined"],[["$","div",null,{"className":"command-grid reference-command","children":[["$","section","United States",{"className":"command-column #315f8c","children":[["$","h3",null,{"children":"United States"}],[["$","div","V Corps, Army of the Potomac-Maj. Gen. Nathaniel P. Banks",{"className":"command-entry","children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"V Corps, Army of the Potomac"}],["$","span",null,{"children":"Maj. Gen. Nathaniel P. Banks"}],["$","small",null,{"children":"Retained overall responsibility for the Valley force but left Winchester for Washington after receiving reassuring reports on March 23"}]]}],["$","div","Second Division, V Corps-Brig. Gen. James Shields",{"className":"command-entry","children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"Second Division, V Corps"}],["$","span",null,{"children":"Brig. Gen. James Shields"}],["$","small",null,{"children":"Wounded on March 22; received battlefield reports in Winchester and sent discretionary suggestions while Kimball commanded in the field"}]]}],["$","div","First Brigade and acting field commander-Col. Nathan Kimball",{"className":"command-entry","children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"First Brigade and acting field commander"}],["$","span",null,{"children":"Col. Nathan Kimball"}],["$","small",null,{"children":"14th Indiana, 8th Ohio, 67th Ohio, and 84th Pennsylvania; concentrated the defense at Pritchard’s Hill and controlled the division on the battlefield"}]]}],["$","div","Second Brigade, Shields’s division-Col. Jeremiah C. Sullivan",{"className":"command-entry","children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"Second Brigade, Shields’s division"}],["$","span",null,{"children":"Col. Jeremiah C. Sullivan"}],["$","small",null,{"children":"5th Ohio, 13th Indiana, 62nd Ohio, and 39th Illinois; the 39th Illinois is listed by NPS as unengaged"}]]}],["$","div","Third Brigade, Shields’s division-Col. Erastus B. Tyler",{"className":"command-entry","children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"Third Brigade, Shields’s division"}],["$","span",null,{"children":"Col. Erastus B. Tyler"}],["$","small",null,{"children":"7th Ohio, 7th Indiana, 1st Virginia (later West Virginia), 29th Ohio, and 110th Pennsylvania; his main attack occurs after this episode ends"}]]}],["$","div","Division artillery-Lt. Col. Philip Daum",{"className":"command-entry","children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"Division artillery"}],["$","span",null,{"children":"Lt. Col. Philip Daum"}],["$","small",null,{"children":"United States and Ohio batteries, including ten rifled pieces concentrated on Pritchard’s Hill during the opening phase"}]]}]]]}],["$","section","Confederacy",{"className":"command-column #9a3d32","children":[["$","h3",null,{"children":"Confederacy"}],["$L1f","$L20","$L21","$L22","$L23","$L24"]]}]]}],"$L25"]]]}]
13:["$","section",null,{"className":"reference-section","id":"images","children":[["$","h2",null,{"children":"Historical images"}],["$","div",null,{"className":"history-gallery","children":[["$","$L26","/images/ep142-nathan-kimball.jpg",{"image":"/images/ep142-nathan-kimball.jpg","alt":"Wartime photographic portrait of United States officer Nathan Kimball in uniform","caption":"Nathan Kimball commanded Shields’s division in the field after Shields was wounded. He used Pritchard’s Hill as the anchor of a cautious defense and correctly anticipated that Ashby’s morning force was not acting alone.","name":"Wartime photographic portrait of United States officer Nathan Kimball in uniform","source":"https://www.loc.gov/pictures/item/2018666446/","credit":"$undefined"}],["$","$L26","/images/ep142-stonewall-jackson.jpg",{"image":"/images/ep142-stonewall-jackson.jpg","alt":"Wartime photographic portrait of Confederate general Thomas J. Stonewall Jackson in uniform","caption":"Jackson reached Kernstown believing he faced a small rear guard. By the episode’s end, he had committed his command and learned that a much larger United States force blocked the route to Winchester.","name":"Wartime photographic portrait of Confederate general Thomas J. Stonewall Jackson in uniform","source":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2003674500/","credit":"$undefined"}],["$","$L26","/images/ep142-kernstown-sneden.jpg",{"image":"/images/ep142-kernstown-sneden.jpg","alt":"Robert Knox Sneden’s color plan of the Battle of Kernstown with roads, terrain, and troop positions","caption":"Robert Knox Sneden’s wartime-era plan presents the battle’s roads, terrain, and colored troop positions. It is a historical interpretation produced between 1862 and 1865, not a surveyed map or a reliable guide to exact strength and frontage. Its troop-count annotation is substantially higher than the official reports and modern estimates.","name":"Robert Knox Sneden’s color plan of the Battle of Kernstown with roads, terrain, a